zefciu <zefi...@Speacock.Pau.Apoznan.Mpl> wrote:
> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

>> If you want to make the game as hard as possible,
>> healers and tourists are the way to go until you
>> conquer their shortfalls, then spread out to the
>> other classes.

> Tourists - yes.  Tourist is hard in the early game
> and gets his main advantage (YPCC) in the late
> game.

And really, for the purposes of the OP, "early game"
is the _whole_ game: getting past dying ridiculously
early is the beginners' goal.

By the time a non-obsessed player is ready to
ascend, the game is seen with a whole different
understanding, and habits learned from playing a
wide variety of races, alignments and classes go
into that first successful ascension.

> But healers have a lot of early game advantages -
> protection racket (it is really easy if you try),

It is really easy once you learn to avoid combat and
bankrupt every shop in the game. Up to then it is a
bit of a mess, but in general, "yes", you're right.

> a big stack of potions

This is the healer's huge advantage. Learning to
quaff all those potions immediately, and so play the
early game with at least 25 and sometims as many as
31 maximum HP puts the healer on a survival par with
any class for the protection racket, and being
inherently poison resistent puts the healer ahead of
several.

> and stone to flesh.

Well, be a bit careful. The protection racket and
Stone to Flesh are mutually exclusive, you normally
don't have enough PW/mana for Stone to Flesh until
XL3, and you want to stay at XL1 for the protection
racket, and, if you're me, for Sokoban as well to
keep the Treasure Zoo within the fighting capacities
of large domestic pets.

> This way we get a character that can get
> great HP, great AC and be almost immune to
> starvation in the early game.  It is just one
> cannot play healer the same way as Valk.

I like to do Sokoban, Mines Town, and if possible
Mines End all at XL1, though going without cloak or
body armor after Mines Town to avoid polymorph traps
destroying those armor items makes the last one
_really_ hard; I'm pretty usually successful at the
first two (modulo some really terrible playing
habits), much less usually successful at the last
one.

The end of the story though is that my healers
starve or faint and are killed _much_ too often,
just because I'm not up to the mana for Stone to
Flesh yet.

Healer's saving grace is definitely starting with
intrinsic poison resistence, which lets them eat
kobolds, centipedes, spiders, snakes, and such; a
varied diet is a larger diet.

What keeps my healers alive at all, though, is
using prayers for food at every other "weak" point,
and so being able to space prayers at least 1200
turns for prayer timeout safety, while usually
accumulating food slowly, between random food, food
death drops, and lichen corpses.

I need to break the habit of gobbling out of fright
in Sokoban, the place my PCs most often starve, so
that I come out with more food than I went in, not
the usual case, sadly.

Mostly, though, you're right, once you've learned
the "run away, run away, let your pets save the day"
habit, healers, initially very hard, become quite
easy, which is why I'm playing human healers almost
exclusively these days, wimp that I am.

